import re

p7_prose = """<p>In 1972, Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart proposed the Levels of Processing (LOP) framework as an alternative to structural multi-store models of human memory. They posited that memory durability is not determined by the duration an item resides in a hypothetical short-term storage buffer, but rather as an automatic byproduct of the depth and qualitative nature of cognitive processing during initial encoding. Cognitive analysis proceeds along a continuous processing hierarchy: from shallow sensory analysis of physical or structural features (e.g., typeface, font size), through intermediate acoustic or phonemic analysis (e.g., rhyming, syllable counting), to deep semantic analysis involving meaning extraction, associative elaboration, and category integration.</p>
<p>Subsequent research by T.B. Rogers and colleagues extended this hierarchy by demonstrating the self-reference effect: evaluating stimuli in relation to one's own self-concept produces retention superior even to standard semantic processing. This advantage arises because the "self" serves as an exceptionally rich, highly organized, and extensively integrated associative schema in long-term memory. However, Morris, Bransford, and Franks later qualified the LOP framework through their principle of Transfer-Appropriate Processing (TAP). They argued that "deep" semantic encoding is not universally superior; rather, memory retrieval is maximized when the cognitive operations engaged during retrieval mirror the specific operations performed during encoding.</p>
<p>To test these frameworks, psychologists presented 120 healthy undergraduates with 80 target nouns across four incidental encoding conditions: Structural ("Is the word printed in capital letters?"), Phonemic ("Does the word rhyme with 'train'?"), Semantic ("Does the word fit the sentence: 'The hunter stalked the ___'?"), and Self-Referential ("Does this trait word describe you?"). After a 20-minute filled arithmetic delay, participants completed either an unannounced standard Free Recall Test or a Rhyme-Cued Recognition Test. Functional neuroimaging recorded mean BOLD signal changes in the left inferior prefrontal cortex (Brodmann areas 45/47) during encoding (Table 1).</p>"""

p_texts = re.findall(r"<p>(.*?)</p>", p7_prose, re.DOTALL)
prose = re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", " ".join(p_texts))
words = prose.split()
print("Word count:", len(words))
